---
title: Dolmades (Stuffed Vine Leaves)
course: Main
servings: 6
prep time: 1h
cook time: 5h
locale: en
difficulty: Medium
cuisine: Greek
---

If @?fresh vine leaves{80} are used, rinse them in cold water and blanch in boiling water for ~{3%minutes} in 3 or 4 lots. Remove to a basin of cold water using a slotted spoon, and then place into a colander to drain. Alternatively, use @preserved vine leaves{500%g}.

Gently fry the @onion{1.5%medium}(finely chopped) in @sunflower oil{3%tbsp} until soft. Add the @lamb mince{1%kg}, breaking up lumps, and fry until browned (optional unless slow cooking on low). Lightly mix the lamb, @rice{150%g}, onion and oil, @?pine nuts{}, @parsley{3%tbsp}(finely chopped), @mint{3%tsp}(finely chopped), @salt{2%tsp}, and @black pepper{}(freshly ground) until well combined.

Place a vine leaf, shiny side down, on the work surface. Snip off the stem if necessary. Place about a tablespoon of meat mixture near the stem end. Fold the end and sides over the stuffing and roll up firmly. Stuffing is sufficient for about 48 dolmades.

Line base of a heavy pan with 6 leaves (use damaged ones) and pack the dolmades close together in layers. Sprinkle each layer with a little of the @lemon juice{3%tbsp}.

Cover the top of the dolmades with the remaining vine leaves. Add the @butter{1%tbsp}, @stock{670%ml}, and any remaining lemon juice. Invert a heavy plate on top to keep the dolmades in shape during cooking.

Cover the pan with a lid and place over medium heat. Bring to a slow simmer, then simmer gently over low heat for ~simmer{1.5%hours}. Alternatively, slow cook for 3 to ~slow cook high{4%hours} on high, or 4 to ~slow cook low{7.5%hours} on low.